<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baldwin of Alna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1243">1243</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Baldwin of Aulne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baldwin of Alna was a Cistercian monk sent by the pope as legate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> around 1230. He tried to bring the newly conquered Baltic lands under direct papal authority rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, making treaties with Curonian leaders who accepted baptism. He created a bishopric for the Curonians in 1234 and briefly held Semigallia, but his plans failed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baldwin, called Baudoin d'Aulne in French, was a monk of Aulne Abbey, a Cistercian house in the Bishopric of Liège, and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> first as envoy and later as legate in the Baltic. In 1230 Cardinal Otto of Tonengo sent him to the Archbishopric of Riga to settle the dispute that followed the death of Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a> in January 1229, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-suerbeer">Albert Suerbeer</a>, named by Archbishop Gerhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and Nicholas of Nauen, chosen by the cathedral chapter. Baldwin reached Riga in July 1230, and by October Otto had decided for Nicholas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the winter of 1230–1231 Baldwin persuaded the Curonians to submit to the Bishopric of Riga in return for food during the famine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and Novgorod, but disputes over the division and baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> forced him to flee to the monastery of Daugavgrīva. He left Livonia early in 1232, was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Semigallia with legatine authority over much of Livonia, and was back by 1233, based in Wiek in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>.</p><p>That summer he demanded that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> surrender the castle of Reval, later Tallinn. They refused and defeated him in fighting around August and September 1233, and he excommunicated them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> was now split between Baldwin's side, with the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, the Buxhöveden family, several monasteries and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> and Curonians, and the Sword Brothers with Nicholas and the city of Riga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Some historians have argued that Baldwin meant to make the whole Baltic region an ecclesiastical state; Manfred Hellmann in 1993 rejected this as fanciful speculation, pointing out that papal letters to him dealt mainly with ending the Livonian conflict on terms favourable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 1234 the pope recalled him and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a> in his place. In 1239 Baldwin joined Baudouin de Courtenay on the Barons' Crusade and took on the duties of the archbishop of Vizia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>. He died in 1243.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baldwin of Alna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The papal legate <b>Baldwin of Alna</b> created a bishopric for the Curonians in 1234 after treaties with Curonian leaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a></li><li><blockquote>A papal legate, <b>Baldwin of Alna</b>, briefly tried to reorganise the see in the 1230s.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-semigallia">Bishopric of Semigallia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1230 a ruler called Lamekins made a treaty with the papal legate <b>Baldwin of Alna</b>, accepting baptism in return for keeping his lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/curonians">Curonians</a></li></ul></section>
